<br>

## Using a comma ',' in print()

When you use commas inside a `print()` statement, Python treats each item as a separate argument. 

Here's what that means in simple terms:

- Python will automatically convert each item into a string, if it's not already.
- Python will also add a space between each item when it prints them.




<p style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 10px; border-left: 5px solid #8148B2; display: inline-block;">
    ➡️ This concept is covered in Lesson 4. You can find more details in the full <a href='https://studio.code.org/s/aif2-2025/lessons/4' target='_blank' rel='noopener noreferrer' style='text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;'>lesson plan</a>.
</p>

